{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec from user messages only\n- Derive the spec strictly from user-authored content; do not import assumptions from assistant reasoning, domain priors, or \u201cobvious\u201d implied details.\n- Treat every assistant statement as a hypothesis to validate, never as a source of truth for the spec.\n- Keep the spec explicit and testable: write it in terms of user-stated constraints that can later be checked against the assistant\u2019s behavior.\n\n### 2) Update the spec monotonically as new user shards arrive\n- In incremental/sharded conversations, merge each new user message into the running spec immediately.\n- Later user messages refine, narrow, or add constraints; do not overwrite earlier constraints unless the user explicitly contradicts them.\n- Track the conversation\u2019s current information state at each turn, not just the final target state.\n\n### 3) Compare each assistant turn against the current accumulated spec\n- Judge the assistant against the user requirements available at that moment, not only against the fully solved task.\n- Use delta-based checking: focus on whether the assistant incorporated the newest user constraints or kept optimizing for an outdated interpretation.\n- Evaluate contradiction first, completeness second: first ask whether the assistant violated any user requirement; only then consider omissions.\n\n### 4) Distinguish supported alignment from plausible but unconfirmed reasoning\n- Only credit steps that are directly justified by user-disclosed information.\n- Treat unsupported specifics in the assistant\u2019s plan as divergence, even if they are plausible or lead toward the correct final answer.\n- Separate \u201ccompatible with the user\u201d from \u201cactually confirmed by the user\u201d; aligned work must be grounded in explicit user evidence.\n\n### 5) Watch for premature narrowing and overcommitment\n- Flag moves that lock onto one interpretation, one output shape, or one solution form before the user has fully revealed the task.\n- When later user shards change the target, identify whether earlier narrowing became obsolete and should be discarded rather than appended to.\n- A locally reasonable partial step can still be globally wrong if it bakes in an assumption the user never gave.\n\n### 6) Separate omissions from contradictions\n- Missing a user constraint is not the same as actively violating one; report them distinctly.\n- Only call a missing detail a failure when it actually affects whether the assistant can satisfy the accumulated spec.\n- Avoid penalizing the assistant for not solving unseen or undisclosed parts of a sharded task.\n\n### 7) Preserve useful structure, discard speculative branches\n- Retain correctly inferred relationships or partial progress that are genuinely supported by the conversation.\n- Quarantine anything that depends on unseen shards or unsupported assumptions.\n- In context-edit or S2-style rewrites, keep the stable user-supported core and remove mistaken reasoning that could anchor later turns.\n\n### 8) Prefer compact, mechanically checkable issue statements\n- Explain exactly which user requirement was missed, contradicted, or unsupported, and why that matters for the next response.\n- Write issues in a way that directly supports consistency checking, rather than broad evaluative commentary.\n- When the assistant is answer-shaped but incomplete for the current information state, name the specific unmet constraint instead of just saying the response is incomplete.\n\n### 9) Use trajectory to explain the failure mode\n- Identify whether the error came from premature assumptions, failure to integrate new shards, anchoring after a user update, or overcommitting to a solution shape.\n- Prefer conflict detection over plausibility checks: if a step cannot be justified from disclosed user information, mark it as needing change.\n- Even when the final answer is correct, inspect whether the path to it relied on unsupported reasoning.\n\n### 10) Optimize the analysis for forward progress\n- In sharded conversations, compact around confirmed constraints and validated partial progress so incorrect intermediate reasoning does not contaminate later analysis.\n- Do not demand full completeness before all user information is available; instead, assess whether the assistant is making the best justified move with the current evidence.\n- Favor continuity of user intent over continuity of the assistant\u2019s narrative.\n\n### 11) Keep output-intent and content-details separate\n- First determine whether the assistant is producing the right kind of response for the current information state; then assess exact details.\n- Track structure, format, and level of commitment separately from domain content.\n- If the assistant chooses the wrong response shape too early, treat that as a distinct divergence from simply getting details wrong.",
